module gunbc.plans.host_trust_provisioned_key

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { p, h2, ul, li }
import std.types { String }

// The T2 arm of the host-trust axis of gunbc.plans.physical_machine_boot_program (its trust section
// on PR #15645): a host key provisioned per install, pinned against the installed system, valid
// across boots of THAT install and dead at reinstall. T1 (per-boot key over the console, #15523
// and #15554) is the sibling this note reuses every mechanism from: the checked host-key fields (the section format runner_host_key_observation reads), the
// sealed pin comparison, the refusal arms. This note opens no parallel trust catalog, transport,
// seed path or marker grammar; it names the existing home and the single arm each one gains.
// Design note first: the note itself created no model, witness, live effect, IAM or secret.
// merry-fox-82 approved the note and directed the model and witnesses (dashboard msg_34308ee8);
// this PR carries that code. One shipped rule NARROWS what that approval named: the catalog
// uniqueness rule is scoped to one host and one install attempt, and that narrowing was ACCEPTED
// by side-chat review at 7e4e926b (merry-fox-82, msg_548aac01) - the cited approval message itself
// named the catalog-wide form. No
// live effect, IAM grant or secret is created here either.

fn opening() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "What T2 promises"),
    p(text: "A host that runs an INSTALLED system (the persistence axis arm RunningInstalledSystem of gunbc.host.running_os, the installed arm of the boot plan's RunningOs) keeps one ssh host key across boots, so a pin must outlive the boot that enrolled it. T2 enrolls that pin once, at install time, from the install's own record, and refuses it thereafter for any other installed system of that host. The four promises, exactly: the key is minted per installed system (per host AND per install) and never baked into a reusable image; the secret store and any IAM are a T2 custody choice, not a dependency of installed-host trust, so custody is modelled as an interface with declared arms and one named proposal; there is no trust on first use, an unpinned first contact refuses through the absent-pin arm; the pin binds to the installed-system identity of the persistence axis, and reinstalling invalidates the old pin."),
    p(text: "The interface authority stays where #15645 put it: gunbc.plans.physical_machine_boot_program's trust section (TrustStrategy, HostTrustStrategySelection, EstablishedHostTrust, TrustStrategySelectionRefused, HostTrustTerminalProof). This note is T2's argument, not a rival of that axis. The pin is a KnownHostKeyRow of gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment consumed by gunbc.host.anchored_host_transport, exactly as the axis declares; one catalog, one transport, no second trust system."),
  ]
}

fn contract() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "The pin, the identity it binds, and the comparison"),
    p(text: "The pin is one new provenance arm on the existing HostKeyEnrollmentProvenance coproduct of gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment: ProvisionedInstall carrying (a) the client MAC of the installing boot — machine identity in the receipt's own currency, since the receipt world carries no HostIdentity and one host's install record must never vouch another host (attempt ids are unique across hosts only by convention, and the compare refuses conventions), (b) the provisioning attempt that carried the install, named by the identity its record in gunbc.provisioning.network_install_receipt gives it, and (c) a citation naming the provisioning record: the install's console capture (the announcing line) and the receipt row. The installed system itself is the receipt's read root - the InstalledRootReading the installer read back at install completion - and the join runs at enrolment: the announcement line arrives on that attempt's console, and that attempt's complete receipt is the InstalledToDisk record. The arm names the attempt rather than embedding the receipt structure because of the module graph, not taste: network_install_receipt reaches the catalog home THROUGH fleet_known_hosts_anchor (receipt -> site_pxe_edge_converge -> anchor -> catalog), so importing it back into the catalog would cycle; the comparison home, gunbc.host.anchored_host_transport, can import both, and there the attempt identity the pin carries is matched against the current receipt. The identity is still the receipt's, read from the side of the graph where it can be. No new registry, no parallel row type."),
    p(text: "Why the pin joins the receipt reading while gap P is open: gunbc.host.running_os declares that the installed-system identity of RunningInstalledSystem is grounded when the installed root is read back after provisioning, and cites network_install_receipt InstalledRootReading as that producer (its frontier refuses to invent a placeholder type first). T2 obeys the same rule: it joins the announcement to the complete receipt that carries that reading, and when the axis lands the installed identity type the pin arm follows to that type. One identity for the running-OS terminal and the pin; no fork."),
    p(text: "The consumer comparison mirrors T1 arm for arm. Where #15523 added anchored_host_transport_per_boot(transport, expected boot id, pin, presented line) refusing PerBootPinFromAnotherBoot, T2 adds a per-install comparison: anchored_host_transport_provisioned over the pinned row, the presented key line, and the host's CURRENT install — supplied NOT as a bare receipt but as the sealed CurrentInstallForHost carrier (side-chat P1 at 7e4e926b: a caller handed an older complete receipt matching an older pin could bind a dead key though a newer install superseded both; the carrier is minted only by the selection of the latest complete receipt for that host, MAC bridged to the fleet identity, and until that fold exists production can supply only the unestablished carrier, which refuses and never binds), refusing a row whose installed reference is superseded — new refusal arms alongside the existing AnchoredBindingAnchorAbsent, AnchoredBindingPresentedKeyMismatch and AnchoredBindingHostBindingMismatch. A stale pin is named as stale, not silently demoted to absent; T1 made the same diagnostic choice for the cross-boot pin."),
    p(text: "Strategy selection is not a config fork. The persistence arm of the running-OS terminal chooses which comparison runs: a delivered-artifact boot is judged by the per-boot pin, an installed system by the provisioned row, and each comparison refuses the other's row — a T1 boot line never vouches for an installed contact, and a T2 install row never vouches for a delivered-artifact boot. The same host can hold both kinds of rows over its life (rescue netboot, then reinstall); the catalog keys rows with their provenance and the persistence arm picks."),
  ]
}

fn physics_of_the_key() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Where the private half can be born: the bootstrap physics"),
    p(text: "The key being trusted cannot travel over the transport its pin protects — that transport refuses an absent pin, which is precisely the no-TOFU rule. So the first copy of the private half must arrive by a channel that is not the anchored transport. The channels an install actually has: the SOL console, read-only and host-bound, owned by the provisioning attempt that fired the boot; BMC virtual media, declared only for boards with a verified media adapter (the boot plan's delivery section reads ALTRAD8UD 3.22.x media as UNVERIFIED — the fleet's PXE installs have no such channel); the PXE edge, which serves per-MAC files the whole install LAN can read (gunbc.fleet.site_pxe_edge_converge, the per-client grub path and install binding — per-unit visibility, zero confidentiality); and the autoinstall seed, whose admission keeps it secret-free — gunbc.provisioning.public_nocloud_seed refuses credential-shaped content and its seed_credential_keys list already refuses the two cloud-init fields that would carry a host private key (autoinstall ssh ssh_keys and cc_ssh ssh_keys), which is the corpus refusing T2's naive form on purpose."),
    p(text: "Conclusion, and it is the note's central claim: a control-plane-minted private host key cannot reach a PXE-installed system at install time at all. Its only doors would be a store credential inside the installer (there is none, and the seed's secret-free admission refuses to carry one) or per-install media the boards do not have. Therefore the initial T2 key is minted where it will live: during the install, on the install target, in a fixed late stage that generates the installed system's host key on the target (a chroot generation on /target before first boot, before sshd has ever run with any other key) and announces the PUBLIC half on the install console. The announcement line is T1's grammar (#15523's GUNBC-HOST-KEY line: marker word, key type, base64, fingerprint): one grammar, two consumption policies keyed on the boot that printed it — an install-boot line enrols a ProvisionedInstall row; a runtime-boot line pins per-boot. No second marker grammar."),
    p(text: "This diverges, with stated reason, from the boot note's T2 parenthetical ('requires a secret store; #15329 sketches one'): that parenthetical priced the rotation phase as if it were the initial phase. Initial provisioning needs no store; the store enters only where a trusted transport already exists to carry the key, which is rotation. The custody interface below keeps #15329's Secret Manager sketch as a declared arm and names the proposal."),
    p(text: "Per-install uniqueness is physical, not procedural. The mint stage runs once, inside the install of a machine that will not be re-captured; the artifact carries the stage and never key material, so a cloned image cannot carry a shared key because no image ever holds one. A DISK clone (an operator act outside this design) still refuses: pins are interpreted against host identity and the host's current install attestation, so the same key meeting another host is a host-binding or mismatch refusal, and the enrollment fold refuses any second row carrying a public half already present in the catalog (the RED below) — today that duplicate check does not exist and the ScannedFirstContact cross-check was manual prose."),
  ]
}

fn custody() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Custody as an interface, and the named proposal"),
    p(text: "HostKeyCustody is a T2-declared coproduct; nothing in installed-host trust (pin, comparison, enrollment) depends on which arm a host uses."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "HostCustodied — PROPOSED for initial provisioning, and sufficient by itself. The private half exists only on the installed root at the standard sshd path, root-owned mode 0600, born on the target during the install. No store, no IAM, no secret creation, no private material on any network. Enrolment cites the console announcement and the install receipt; revocation authority lives in the enrollment catalog, not in the key file: superseding the row makes the key untrusted fleet-wide whether or not the file is erased."),
      li(text: "StoreCustodied — the ROTATION mechanism, and the #15329 shape kept as a declared arm. A per-host-install lane row of gunbc.host.fleet_keypair_mint (the existing single mint policy: tmpfs mint directory refused without the fstype check, redaction of the private material from every return, the receipt recording public line and fingerprint in one commit — mint is account creation per gunbc.design_ruling the_mint_of_a_fleet_ssh_keypair_is_account_creation, never a human ceremony), the secret living in Secret Manager through gunbc.secret_provision_actuator exactly as mtcollins1_ssh_host_key_secret does, and placement through a row added to gunbc.fleet.host_credential_custody_converge whose medium is CustodyDiskBacked (the installed root), contrasting the Mtcollins1SshHostKey arm whose TmpfsNoSwap medium is right for a RAM-boot host and wrong for an installed one."),
      li(text: "Rotation dance — legal only because the current pin already anchors the transport: mint a StoreCustodied copy; enrol the new row BESIDE the old (a deliberate dual-pin window); place the key at the installed path over the still-trusted anchored transport (the existing custody converge legs consume gunbc.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor's file); supersede the old row; destroy the retired secret version. Guest-side rotation (re-mint in the guest at a maintenance boot, re-announce on console, fold supersedes) is the same lifecycle with no store at all."),
      li(text: "REJECTED arm — escrow. Storing a copy of the initial private half for disaster recovery buys re-placement of a key whose install is already gone, which is exactly the cross-install reuse the pin's install binding exists to refuse. Recovery is reinstall, and reinstall mints fresh."),
    ]),
    p(text: "The mtcollins1 literal (mtcollins1_ssh_host_key_secret, mtcollins1_host_key_anchor, the custody arm) is a stable key on ephemeral media enrolled from a transcription: IndependentlyAnchored, neither T1 nor T2 — it demonstrates the custody and receipt grammar T2's rotation arm reuses, and T2 does not absorb it. The live frontier that row carries (sshd not yet started with the key, no live route through anchored_host_transport_of) is the transport frontier, shared by both strategies."),
  ]
}

fn lifecycle() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Mint, enrol, use, rotate, revoke, reinstall"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "MINT — during the install transaction, on the install target: the fixed seed stage arm generates the installed system's host key on /target. One key, one install, born on the machine that keeps it."),
      li(text: "ENROL — the install console carries the public line inside the capture envelope the attempt already owns (host identity plus the install boot's HostBootId from gunbc.host.host_boot_id); the install receipt joins that boot to the installed root. The enrollment fold admits the ProvisionedInstall row under the constructor, duplicate and staleness rules below. Ordering obligation: enrolment lands BEFORE the boot transaction's trust terminal, so the fleet's first contact with the installed system is already a pinned comparison — the absent-pin arm (AnchoredBindingAnchorAbsent, ProbeHostKeyUnenrolled in gunbc.host.host_reach_identity_probe) is what makes 'first use' never trusted."),
      li(text: "USE — the per-install comparison binds key to host to install and yields the EstablishedHostTrust the initialized-host record (gunbc.host.initialized_host, terminal of the boot transaction) carries; gunbc.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor materializes the catalog, T2 rows included, into the content-bound anchor file real ssh calls consume, including the custody legs."),
      li(text: "ROTATE — either arm of the custody section; both end in a fold supersede, and the store arm's dual-pin window is the only state where a host carries two pins, declared as such."),
      li(text: "REVOKE — supersede or remove the row in the catalog (the fold's authority); the stale row stops being authoritative and every comparison treats the host as unenrolled. For StoreCustodied rows the retired secret version is also destroyed; erasing a HostCustodied file is optional hygiene, not the revocation."),
      li(text: "REINSTALL — a fresh install transaction mints a fresh key and announces a fresh line; the fold supersedes the prior row for that host. Reinstall WITHOUT re-enrolment refuses twice over: the reinstalled guest presents a new key that mismatches the stale pin, and even if the key were somehow reused (the StoreCustodied failure mode this design forbids), the row's installed reference no longer matches the host's latest complete install receipt, which is the named stale-install refusal. Failure is honest: no console line at install means no pin and the boot transaction's trust terminal fails closed — there is no silent fallback to T1, because the per-boot comparison refuses installed contact by construction."),
    ]),
    p(text: "Duplicate or missed announcements, mirroring fleet_keypair_mint's unknown-outcome guidance: a duplicate public half in two announcements meets the fold's duplicate refusal; a missed announcement means no pin, and every contact refuses. Neither is papered over."),
  ]
}

fn homes() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Homes touched — one arm each, nothing parallel"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment — +1 provenance arm (ProvisionedInstall: the installing boot's client MAC, the installing attempt's identity, and the provisioning citation; the two strings are NonEmptyStr, so the unnamed state has no constructor); catalog uniqueness of a public half by host identity — one attempt may own it, one (address, record) slot may state it, multi-address rows and corroborating records for one host's one key are legal — and supersession against the latest complete install receipt, evaluated in gunbc.host.anchored_host_transport where the receipt can be imported (the catalog home sits upstream of it in the module graph). Its wet catalog-row path already exists in this home."),
      li(text: "gunbc.host.anchored_host_transport — the per-install comparison function and its refusal arms (superseded install, unattested install, pin not from an install record, unestablished current-install carrier; the carrier type itself lives in gunbc.provisioning.network_install_receipt beside the selection fold that will mint it); the pin's only mint stays gunbc.host.per_boot_console_pin per_boot_pin_from_checked_key over checked_host_key (#15523 deleted the line grammar); the install announcement reaches it through the same section format runner_host_key_observation reads (gunbc.runner.runner_host_medium), arriving on the install boot: two consumption policies, never a forked grammar."),
      li(text: "gunbc.provisioning.ubuntu_autoinstall_seed — the closed schema gains ONE fixed arm declaring the install-time host-key stage (generate on target; announce the public half). The arm carries no free string, no locator, no bytes; the public seed's credential-shaped backstop keeps refusing ssh_keys-style content, and its seed_credential_keys list already names both host-key-carrying fields."),
      li(text: "gunbc.provisioning.network_install_receipt — unchanged; it is the producer of the installed identity (InstalledRootReading) and of install completion the fold joins announcements to."),
      li(text: "gunbc.host.running_os and gunbc.host.initialized_host (gap P, quiet-gull-101 line) — the installed identity type lands there; the pin arm follows it. No T2 placeholder type is coined first."),
      li(text: "gunbc.host.fleet_keypair_mint, gunbc.secret_provision_actuator, gunbc.fleet.host_credential_custody_converge — rotation-lane rows only (CustodyDiskBacked medium), declared, not built by the initial slice."),
      li(text: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor and gunbc.host.host_reach_identity_probe — unchanged consumers that already read the catalog; T2 rows flow through them automatically."),
    ]),
    p(text: "Consumers of the pin, named: anchored_host_transport's per-install comparison (the typed comparison every host-directed leg rides); host_reach_identity_probe's expectation lookup (enrolled_fingerprint_for); the initialized-host terminal record; fleet_known_hosts_anchor's materialized file for real ssh calls, which custody converge rides."),
  ]
}

fn reds() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "REDs the design owes (model-level witnesses, no live effect)"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Cloned-key reuse refuses — dag/test/claim/fleet/fleet_host_key_enrollment_witness.dag: the catalog rule fleet_host_key_public_halves_are_unique refuses a second row sharing a public half unless the rows name the SAME host and, where both are ProvisionedInstall records, the SAME install attempt: a half enrolled for a different host (the clone case) refuses, and a half enrolled twice for one host under different install attempts (reinstall reuse) refuses. One host's one install may carry the same half at several addresses or in corroborating records — the registry holds such rows today (srv1's tailnet row and its ts.net row share a half). The rule compares public halves; a fingerprint is derived from the half, so it is not compared separately. This fold-level uniqueness rule does not exist before this PR; it is the named new rule. Its scoped form NARROWS the catalog-wide rule the note's approval named; the narrowing was accepted by side-chat review at 7e4e926b (merry-fox-82)."),
      li(text: "Supplying an older receipt cannot bind — dag/test/claim/host/anchored_host_transport_witness.dag: the comparison consumes the sealed CurrentInstallForHost carrier, never a bare receipt. With installs A and B both complete and B current in the carrier, pin A and key A refuse as superseded; a carrier whose association with the transport's host is unestablished refuses even when MAC, attempt and key all match (a_current_install_carrier_for_another_host_never_binds); and until the latest-complete-install selection fold exists — the carrier's only intended producer, its Established arm sole_constructor-sealed and admit_callers-gated to these witnesses — the only carrier production can supply is the unestablished one, which refuses (an_unestablished_current_install_never_binds_a_provisioned_pin)."),
      li(text: "Constructor admission refuses — same home: a ProvisionedInstall row lacking the installed reference or the provisioning citation does not exist."),
      li(text: "Reinstall without re-enrolment refuses — both dag/test/claim/fleet/fleet_host_key_enrollment_witness.dag and dag/test/claim/host/anchored_host_transport_witness.dag: against a newer complete install receipt the old row is not authoritative (a presented key matching the old row meets the stale-install refusal), and the fresh-key case meets AnchoredBindingPresentedKeyMismatch."),
      li(text: "Another machine's install record never vouches a pin — dag/test/claim/host/anchored_host_transport_witness.dag: a complete current receipt whose install-boot client MAC differs from the MAC the pin names refuses as not-attested, even when the attempt id and the presented key match; host equality is enforced NOW, in the receipt's own currency, because the receipt world carries no HostIdentity and the persistence axis that bridges install-subject to fleet identity is the axis's, not this compare's."),
      li(text: "Pin mismatch refuses, and absent pins refuse — existing arms gain a row for the ProvisionedInstall provenance: an unpinned first contact to a just-installed system is refused before any trust."),
      li(text: "Strategy crossing refuses both ways — the per-boot comparison rejects a provisioned row for a delivered-artifact boot; the per-install comparison rejects a per-boot pin for an installed contact."),
      li(text: "The seed admits the stage and refuses the key — the seed home's witness admits the fixed stage arm and refuses host-key bytes or locators smuggled into it, alongside the existing seed_credential_keys refusals of autoinstall ssh ssh_keys and cc_ssh ssh_keys."),
    ]),
    p(text: "The rotation lane, if and when it is built, carries its own mint and custody witnesses at those homes' claims; they are not double-counted here."),
  ]
}

fn operator_asks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "What the operator actually does"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Chosen initial slice (HostCustodied): NO new IAM, NO secret created, NO container. The operator's asks are the ordinary landing of the model changes (seed schema arm, enrollment arm and fold rules, transport comparison), and the per-host trust-strategy policy the axis already declares a true unknown — which hosts run installed and therefore take T2. Dispatching an install is already an operator-gated act; the mint rides inside that gate, adding no new one."),
      li(text: "StoreCustodied rotation lane (a later, optional arm): creating the per-host-install secret container is operator-approval through gunbc.secret_provision_actuator; dispatching the mint is go through the fleet_keypair_mint pattern; applying the accessor IAM grant for the custody route belongs to the fleet_secret_accessor_roster's IAM homes. These asks are T2-arm asks; installed-host trust in general does not carry them."),
    ]),
  ]
}

fn unknowns() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "True unknowns and declared edges"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Whether every install-eligible fleet host carries a console capture usable through the whole install boot is unread (the C battery line, cool-owl-599's work). T2 without a console is not implementable for that host — the honest consequence is a TrustStrategySelectionRefused-shaped operator decision for that host, not a fallback."),
      li(text: "The installer's own ssh exposure during the install boot (subiquity's toggle, the install VLAN) is a live exposure unrelated to the pin; this note records it and does not claim it closed."),
      li(text: "The announcement's placement inside the closed seed schema is the seed home's schema-extension decision (one fixed arm, no free strings); this note asks for it and names its refusals, it does not pre-design the seed's internals."),
      li(text: "gap P may retype the pin's installed reference when the axis lands the installed identity; that edit belongs to the axis and T2 follows — the one-way door is the identity's producer (the install receipt reading), already common to both."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data host_trust_provisioned_key_plan: Plan = Plan {
  slug: "host-trust-provisioned-install-key",
  title: "Host trust T2: a provisioned per-install host key - minted where it lives, pinned against the install, rotated over the trust it already protects",
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly,
  body: concat(concat(concat(concat(concat(concat(concat(concat(
    opening(),
    contract()),
    physics_of_the_key()),
    custody()),
    lifecycle()),
    homes()),
    reds()),
    operator_asks()),
    unknowns()),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen {
    condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Retires when installed fleet hosts' first and continuing contacts ride only anchored_host_transport comparisons over ProvisionedInstall rows, the stale-install refusal is green at its witness, and the boot axis's trust-selection row records which hosts take T2; if the axis retires the provisioned strategy instead, this note retires with it."),
  },
}
